{% doc %}
  Renders a hero video capped at a resolution ceiling.

  Shopify's `video_tag` filter emits a single `<source>`, always the highest
  rendition it has. For the homepage montage that is a 1080p / 4.8 Mbps file of
  roughly 13.4 MB, served identically to a 390px phone and a 1440px desktop.
  There is no declarative way to fix that downstream: `<source media="...">` is
  ignored inside `<video>` by every browser, so the choice has to be made here,
  server side.

  Shopify transcodes each upload into a rendition ladder and exposes it as
  `video.sources`, so this usually costs nothing but a smaller pick. The same
  hand-rolled `<source>` pattern is used by `snippets/background-media.liquid`.

  Falls back to `video_tag` unchanged when the ladder has no rendition at or
  below the ceiling, so a video that only ever transcoded to 1080p still renders.

  @param {object} video - the Shopify video object to render
  @param {string} [class] - classes applied to the `<video>` element
  @param {string} [testid] - value for `data-testid` on the `<video>` element
  @param {number} [max_height] - resolution ceiling in pixels, defaults to 720
  @param {number} [poster_width] - width of the poster derivative, defaults to 600
  @param {boolean} [preload_poster] - preload the poster at high priority; set this
    only for the video that is actually the LCP element, never for offscreen slides

  @example
  {% render 'hero-video',
    video: section.settings.video_1,
    class: 'hero__video hero__video--desktop',
    poster_width: 1440,
    testid: 'hero-desktop-video-1'
  %}
{% enddoc %}

{% liquid
  assign height_ceiling = max_height | default: 720

  # `video.preview_image` is the thumbnail Shopify generates during transcoding,
  # which is the video's own first frame unless a custom one was uploaded in
  # Admin. That makes it the right poster here: it paints as soon as a small
  # image lands rather than after the first frame of a multi-megabyte file
  # decodes, and the handover to playback is seamless because the pixels match.
  assign poster_derivative_width = poster_width | default: 600
  assign poster_url = ''
  assign poster_rendered_width = 0
  assign poster_rendered_height = 0
  if video.preview_image != blank
    assign poster_url = video.preview_image | image_url: width: poster_derivative_width
    # Dimensions of the derivative actually served, not of the original. image_url
    # never upscales, so a preview image narrower than the requested width comes
    # back at its own size. Declaring the original's width against a downscaled
    # src would describe a file that is not the one being fetched.
    assign poster_rendered_width = poster_derivative_width
    if video.preview_image.width < poster_derivative_width
      assign poster_rendered_width = video.preview_image.width
    endif
    assign poster_rendered_height = poster_rendered_width | times: video.preview_image.height | divided_by: video.preview_image.width
  endif

  # Pick the tallest mp4 rendition at or under the ceiling. Tracking the height
  # in a scalar rather than holding the source object avoids comparing an object
  # against blank, which Liquid does not do usefully.
  assign selected_url = ''
  assign selected_height = 0

  for source in video.sources
    if source.mime_type == 'video/mp4' and source.height <= height_ceiling and source.height > selected_height
      assign selected_url = source.url
      assign selected_height = source.height
    endif
  endfor
%}

{%- if preload_poster and poster_url != blank -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    A `poster` is fetched at Low priority and cannot carry `fetchpriority`, so on a
    throttled connection it queues behind everything else: measured at 1529 ms of
    load delay for a file that downloads in under a millisecond. This preload
    promotes it without changing what gets fetched. It resolves to the same URL as
    the attribute below, from the same variable, so the two coalesce into one
    request rather than racing.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <link
    rel="preload"
    as="image"
    fetchpriority="high"
    href="{{ poster_url }}"
  >
{%- endif -%}

{%- if selected_url != blank -%}
  <video
    playsinline
    autoplay
    loop
    muted
    preload="metadata"
    {% if poster_url != blank %}
      poster="{{ poster_url }}"
    {% endif %}
    class="{{ class }}"
    {% if testid != blank %}
      data-testid="{{ testid }}"
    {% endif %}
  >
    <source src="{{ selected_url }}" type="video/mp4">
    {%- if video.preview_image != blank -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        No-video-support fallback, not a poster: a child `<img>` inside `<video>`
        renders only when the browser cannot play the source, so on every modern
        browser this is downloaded and never shown. Keep it small. `video_tag`
        emits Shopify's tiny `_small` thumbnail variant here; requesting a large
        derivative instead costs every mobile visitor tens of KB of bandwidth
        early in the load, competing with the video itself, for a frame nobody
        sees.

        `video_tag` also emits this element with no alt at all, which is the
        origin of the image-alt finding in THEME_CHECK_NON_ACTIONABLE.md.
        Emitting it ourselves lets us set one.

        Points at the same derivative as the `poster` above rather than a width of
        its own, so the two never cost two separate downloads.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <img
        src="{{ poster_url }}"
        width="{{ poster_rendered_width }}"
        height="{{ poster_rendered_height }}"
        alt="{{ video.alt | default: '' | escape }}"
      >
    {%- endif -%}
  </video>
{%- else -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    No rendition at or below the ceiling. Render exactly what this section
    rendered before, rather than dropping the video.

    Branched on the poster rather than passing `poster: poster_url` in one call:
    an empty string would emit `poster=""`, which resolves against the document
    and makes the browser fetch the page itself as an image.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if poster_url != blank -%}
    {{
      video
      | video_tag:
        poster: poster_url,
        autoplay: true,
        loop: true,
        controls: false,
        muted: true,
        class: class,
        data-testid: testid
    }}
  {%- else -%}
    {{
      video
      | video_tag:
        poster: nil,
        autoplay: true,
        loop: true,
        controls: false,
        muted: true,
        class: class,
        data-testid: testid
    }}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endif -%}
